Chinese and 9 Indian). Although the participants did not live with their parents, 40.2% of them had at least one living parent. Malay Paired Samples t-Test, the results showed that the mean score of 6 coping methods including active coping (t=-5.85, P<0.001), of emotional supports(t =-4.80, P<0.001), of reframing(t=-3.89, P<0.001 ), planning(t=-4.91, P<0.001) and acceptance (t=-5.98, P<0.001) methods significantly increased after interventional sessions. Meanwhile, the mean score of 3 coping methods including selfdenial   (t 2.80, P<0.02) and self-blame (t=7.24, P 0.001) significantly decreased in post-test. No significant change was found in mean score of substance use, behavior disengagement, venting; humor and religion (p>0.05). Using using using","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":146.25331624348962,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":54.333333333333336,"r":286.6666666666667,"t":232.91998291015625},"charspan":[0,242],"page_no":1},{"bbox":{"b":152.25331624348962,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":309.3333333333333,"r":541.0,"t":386.21705786307007},"charspan":[243,868],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/9","text":"Most of the participants in both groups were female (19 females VS. 11 males) with ethnicity (14 Malay vs. 7 Chinese and 9 Indian). Although the participants did not live with their parents, 40.2% of them had at least one living parent. Malay Paired Samples t-Test, the results showed that the mean score of 6 coping methods including active coping (t=-5.85, P<0.001), of emotional supports(t =-4.80, P<0.001), of reframing(t=-3.89, P<0.001 ), planning(t=-4.91, P<0.001) and acceptance (t=-5.98, P<0.001) methods significantly increased after interventional sessions. Meanwhile, the mean score of 3 coping methods including selfdenial   (t 2.80, P<0.02) and self-blame (t=7.24, P 0.001) significantly decreased in post-test. No significant change was found in mean score of substance use, behavior disengagement, venting; humor and religion (p>0.05). Using usin